{"duration_api_ms":145428,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9effdb4b-2adc-4f7d-93fe-19f52e14f930","total_cost_usd":0.21477759999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9289,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":17671,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":12791},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9289,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":17671,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9289},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":17671,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9289,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.21477759999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":12791,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\n\\nAuthor: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\\n\\n### Issue Description\\n\\nAppend requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`. Callers see a write that will not land: Nimbrel Relay stalls, batch loaders resend the same envelope, and reads against the same shard begin to time out. The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\\n\\nOn one managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment (MLS), the share of HTTP 429 replies climbed over a fortnight, as counted at the front proxy; front proxy counters agreed with client-side reports, confirming the refusals were real rather than a client-side accounting error. A 429 is what the Ledger returns when it refuses work outright rather than queueing it. Investigation pointed to expensive queries and deep grouping as contributing factors, addressed under Cause below.\\n\\nA full refusal envelope from a batched append reads like this:\\n\\n```\\nappend batch refused after 0 of 64 envelopes: 429 Too Many Requests: {\\\"error\\\":{\\\"root_cause\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"refused append on ingest path [ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344, follower_bytes=212992, all_bytes=88334336, ingest_op_bytes=155648, max_ingest_bytes=88080384]\\\"}],\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"refused append on ingest path [ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344, follower_bytes=212992, all_bytes=88334336, ingest_op_bytes=155648, max_ingest_bytes=88080384]\\\"},\\\"status\\\":429}\\n```\\n\\n> **NOTE:** The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\\n\\n### Environment\\n\\nEvery Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way. One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\\n\\n### Cause\\n\\nA [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\\n\\nThere are 3 ways an append draws a 429:\\n\\n- The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\\n- The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`). [1]\\n- A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\\n\\n[1] **From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward**, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\\n\\nContributing factors seen in one worker-pool exhaustion case included expensive queries and deep grouping: a handful of queries ran for as long as 2 seconds each, and a worker slot was unavailable for as long as one of them held it, so the queue behind it grew. Grouping on `channel.id` produced a very large number of groups, and the memory that cost was charged to the same pool the append path draws on. Processor load looked unremarkable while memory sat high, which pointed at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\\n\\n### Workaround\\n\\nTake append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\\n\\nWhere batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, send fewer envelopes per batch.\\n\\nWhere expensive queries are contributing to the refusals: lower the slow query threshold to 1 second so that the queries actually responsible are recorded instead of averaged away; review the expensive queries with the team that wrote them, since several may be able to ask for less; and capture a thread dump and a heap snapshot while the refusal rate is high, rather than reasoning from counters alone.\\n\\n### Resolution\\n\\nA cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out). Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\\n\\nSplitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\\n\\nFor `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later:\\n\\n1. Begin by cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\\n2. Where smaller batches do not clear it, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\\n3. Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\\n   A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\\n   The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\\n\\nWhere none of that is open to you, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both. Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\\n\\n### References\\n\\n{internal-notes}\\n\\n- [Internal ticket NB-40917](https://tickets.nimbrel.example/issues/40917)\\n\\n{/internal-notes}\\n\\n- [Ledger slow query log](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/slow-query-log)\\n- [HTTP 429 replies](https://support.nimbrel.example/knowledge/318kqp2)\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# Rising 429 refusals on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title kept; it names the general issue the b document narrows.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"author/updated byline\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"Author: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"Author: Devin Okonkwo\\nUpdated: 2026-04-11\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"Author: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's authorship metadata retained for the merged article.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\", \"reason\": \"Base title chosen over this one.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b2\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Author: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\", \"reason\": \"Base author/date kept instead.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b3\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"### Issue Description\", \"reason\": \"Summary heading consolidated into base's Issue Description.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b4\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"On one managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment (MLS), the share of HTTP 429 replies climbed over a fortnight, as counted at the front proxy; front proxy counters agreed with client-side reports, confirming the refusals were real rather than a client-side accounting error.\", \"reason\": \"Restated as part of Issue Description, MLS name aligned with Environment.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b5\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Investigation pointed to expensive queries and deep grouping as contributing factors, addressed under Cause below.\", \"reason\": \"Restated to point readers to the merged Cause section.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b6\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"### Environment\", \"reason\": \"Same heading as base's Environment section.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b7\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\", \"reason\": \"Environment attributes folded into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b8\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\", \"reason\": \"Environment attributes folded into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b9\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\", \"reason\": \"Environment attributes folded into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b10\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\", \"reason\": \"Environment attributes folded into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b11\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"One recorded instance occurred on Nimbrel Ledger version 4.4 and 5.x, running on Nimbrel Cloud under the Managed Ledger Service (MLS), in a production environment.\", \"reason\": \"Environment attributes folded into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b12\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"### Issue Description\", \"reason\": \"Same heading as base's Issue Description section.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b13\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"A 429 is what the Ledger returns when it refuses work outright rather than queueing it.\", \"reason\": \"Restated within the merged Issue Description paragraph.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b14\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"front proxy counters agreed with client-side reports, confirming the refusals were real rather than a client-side accounting error.\", \"reason\": \"Folded into the same Issue Description sentence as b4.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b15\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"### Cause\", \"reason\": \"Contributing Factors heading consolidated into base's Cause heading.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b16\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Contributing factors seen in one worker-pool exhaustion case included expensive queries and deep grouping: a handful of queries ran for as long as 2 seconds each, and a worker slot was unavailable for as long as one of them held it, so the queue behind it grew. Grouping on `channel.id` produced a very large number of groups, and the memory that cost was charged to the same pool the append path draws on. Processor load looked unremarkable while memory sat high, which pointed at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\", \"reason\": \"Bullet list turned into a prose addition under Cause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b17\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"a handful of queries ran for as long as 2 seconds each, and a worker slot was unavailable for as long as one of them held it, so the queue behind it grew\", \"reason\": \"Bullet list turned into a prose addition under Cause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b18\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Grouping on `channel.id` produced a very large number of groups, and the memory that cost was charged to the same pool the append path draws on.\", \"reason\": \"Bullet list turned into a prose addition under Cause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b19\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Processor load looked unremarkable while memory sat high, which pointed at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\", \"reason\": \"Bullet list turned into a prose addition under Cause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b20\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Processor load looked unremarkable while memory sat high, which pointed at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\", \"reason\": \"Bullet list turned into a prose addition under Cause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b21\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"### Workaround\", \"reason\": \"Proposed Actions heading consolidated into base's Workaround heading.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b22\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"Where expensive queries are contributing to the refusals: lower the slow query threshold to 1 second so that the queries actually responsible are recorded instead of averaged away; review the expensive queries with the team that wrote them, since several may be able to ask for less; and capture a thread dump and a heap snapshot while the refusal rate is high, rather than reasoning from counters alone.\", \"reason\": \"Proposed Actions bullets folded into a Workaround paragraph.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b23\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"review the expensive queries with the team that wrote them, since several may be able to ask for less\", \"reason\": \"Proposed Actions bullets folded into a Workaround paragraph.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b24\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"capture a thread dump and a heap snapshot while the refusal rate is high, rather than reasoning from counters alone\", \"reason\": \"Proposed Actions bullets folded into a Workaround paragraph.\"}\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\"\n}","ttft_ms":116602,"type":"result","duration_ms":145489,"uuid":"5edaa11f-56e7-4d29-b34a-dc44c3ac79bc","ttft_stream_ms":2079,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":2079,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
